 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Multiscale Hard Voxelization](3319-multiscale-hard-voxelization.md)
-Multiscale Hard Voxelization voxelizes a point cloud into multiple spatial scales simultaneously, outputting voxel features, coordinates, and point counts for each scale. It differs from standard single-scale voxelization by iterating over multiple scales and storing results in separate memory blocks indexed by `scale_idx`, providing convenience for multi-scale feature extraction networks.
+Multiscale Hard Voxelization is a GPU-accelerated method that simultaneously voxelizes a LiDAR point cloud into multiple spatial resolutions (scales) in a single pass. It extends standard hard voxelization by iterating over a list of voxel sizes and point limits, outputting separate voxel feature tensors, coordinates, and point counts for each scale. It differs from single-scale voxelization by managing multiple memory blocks indexed by scale, offering a 10% speed improvement over ordinary single-scale CUDA operators on NVIDIA RTX 3090 hardware.
 
 ### [Hard Voxelization (Deterministic)](3321-hard-voxelization-deterministic.md)
-Hard Voxelization (Deterministic) converts a 3D point cloud into a fixed-size voxel grid by assigning each point to a discrete voxel based on its coordinates and a specified voxel size and range. It handles duplicate points within the same voxel and limits the number of points per voxel and total voxels. Unlike the non-deterministic variant which uses atomic operations for speed at the cost of reproducibility, this method guarantees bit-identical output by using stable sorting and explicit ordering logic. It builds on [Hard Voxelization](5345-hard-voxelization.md).
+Hard Voxelization (Deterministic) discretizes a continuous 3D point cloud into a fixed-size 3D grid of cubic voxels by binning points based on their spatial coordinates and aggregating associated attribute values within each voxel. It outputs a single representative point per occupied voxel calculated as the centroid of the aggregated attributes. Unlike its sibling [Multiscale Hard Voxelization](3319-multiscale-hard-voxelization.md), which handles multiple resolutions, this method operates on a single fixed scale. It differs from the non-deterministic variant by guaranteeing reproducible output through explicit sorting and unique-coordinate identification, ensuring that the order of points within a voxel is preserved, whereas non-deterministic versions rely on atomic operations that may yield varying results across runs.
 
 ### [Point Cloud Voxelization Visualization](5628-point-cloud-voxelization-visualization.md)
-A visualization pipeline that renders voxelized point clouds using the Point Cloud Library (PCL), supporting axis-aligned or custom bounding boxes with three distinct rendering modes. It differs from its siblings by providing immediate visual feedback of voxel occupancy and bounding constraints through specific geometric representations (points, solid cubes, or wireframes) rather than just generating the underlying data structure.
+This method visualizes the occupancy of a point cloud after voxelization using the Point Cloud Library (PCL). It supports axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABB) or custom fixed-resolution grids, and offers three rendering modes: point cloud coloring, solid cubes, or wireframe cubes. It differs from standard hard voxelization by focusing on the visual representation of voxel centers and occupancy rather than producing a dense tensor for deep learning.
 
